WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
14 January 2020. A dark globe floats in black space, with Asia and Australia facing us. Carbon dioxide shows as a pale tan haze; where it is densest it glows orange-red, here over eastern China. You hear a soft breath and a slow heartbeat, one beat per model day.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.500
The globe turns slowly as the days tick by in the lower right corner, one hour of model time per frame. The orange-red over China pulses and drifts out over the Pacific. Oboe for Asia, high, and harp for Oceania, low, follow how much orange-red is in their part of the picture.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.000
Late January. India and the Indian Ocean come round, and the east coast of Africa appears on the left. A flute for Europe and a marimba for Africa join in, while the oboe and harp fade out.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Early February. Africa fills the globe. The Sahara and Arabia are wrapped in light grey-tan haze, and a band of orange-red flickers across Africa south of the Sahara and along the Gulf of Guinea, where fires burn. The pulsing comes and goes with day and night.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Mid February. West Africa slides to the right edge and the bulge of South America rises on the left, already glowing orange-red.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Around 20 February. South America faces us. Almost the whole continent is covered in a pulsing orange-red mesh, densest over the Amazon and Brazil. Vibraphone for North America, high, and cello for South America, low, enter as Europe and Africa fade away.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Late February. North and Central America come into view at the top. Swirls of tan haze curl over the North Atlantic and the United States, carried by storm systems, while South America keeps pulsing below.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.200
Early March 2020. The Americas sit in the centre of the globe, the haze still swirling over the north. The breath, heartbeat and the two American voices play to the end.
